Compatibility is a relationship among artifacts and observations, not a property of a release in isolation. A new compiler may accept old source yet bind a name differently. An existing binary may link to a changed library but produce a new result. Serialized data, bytecode, and debugging formats may outlive the tool that wrote them. Before declaring a change compatible, say which artifacts can be reused and what must remain true about them.

\section{Separate Source, Binary, and Behavioral Promises}

Bjarne Stroustrup's \emph{Evolving a Language in and for the Real World: C++ 1991--2006} describes standardization of a widely used language under pressure to support new idioms without fragmenting existing practice. Supporting a new generic technique involved changes to library practice as well as core language design. An installed community is not one frozen set of programs: compilers, independent libraries, build tools, and style conventions move at different speeds. Guy L. Steele Jr. and Richard P. Gabriel's \emph{The Evolution of Lisp} traces another route through projects and dialects that split, rejoin, and influence one another. Experimentation can happen outside a common standard, but dialect differences still impose a cost on portable clients.

For source compatibility ask whether unchanged source is accepted by the new implementation. For binary compatibility ask whether artifacts built against an old interface still work together under the promised link and load rules. For behavior compare specified observable events for clients whose source and dependencies are admitted. Passing the first two tests does not establish the third.

\section{Worked Example: Adding an Overload}

\emph{The Java Language Specification}, Java SE 19 edition, by James Gosling and colleagues, explains in its section on method and constructor overloading why existing binaries keep their chosen signatures. Consider a Java library that initially offers \verb|static String pick(Object v)| returning \texttt{"general"}. A client prints the result of \verb|Picks.pick("north")|. Version two adds \verb|static String pick(CharSequence v)| returning \texttt{"text"}. Both methods have the same accessibility and the old one remains unchanged.

\begin{verbatim}
Client artifact                 Library v1   Library v2
compiled against v1             general      general
rebuilt against v2              n/a          text
\end{verbatim}

The binary built against v1 already refers to the overload with an \texttt{Object} parameter. On recompilation both overloads apply and \texttt{CharSequence} is more specific. Neither client fails to build against its stated version, and the old client still links to v2; nevertheless, rebuilding changes the observable output. The missing comparison is informative too: running the newly compiled client against v1, when it tries to invoke the new descriptor, fails under ordinary Java linkage. If a client must keep the previous selection on rebuild, it can write \verb|Picks.pick((Object) "north")|. A migration checker can find callers whose resolved overloads differ and suggest such a change where the old method remains callable. A diff of exported names would miss the change because the method name was already present.

\section{Make New Modes Explicit and Test Them Together}

Steve Klabnik and Carol Nichols's \emph{The Rust Programming Language}, in its appendix on editions, describes opt-in language changes and compiling dependencies written for different editions together. The useful separation is between selecting a front-end interpretation for a source unit and selecting a compatible interface for its clients. It does not imply that arbitrary artifacts from different toolchains are mutually linkable. If a new edition reserves a word, migrate existing identifiers according to their bindings rather than globally replacing a string that may also occur in comments, data, or external names.

Set a release invariant: upgrading the toolchain alone must not switch a package's declared edition, and editing the edition must run the project through an explicit compatibility check. Test old sources, old binaries where supported, fresh builds, and mixed-edition dependency graphs. For an automatic migration, compare resolved public references and observable behavior before and after; keep diagnostics as reviewable spans rather than trusting a successful build. The \href{dossier-on-modern-cc-design/chapters/20-versioned-ir.md}{dossier's Versioned IR chapter} makes the analogous point for compiler internals: readers of a persistent representation need an explicit version contract, not an assumption that today's interpretation is yesterday's.
